## Discussion: i.ar future and the "Laboratory" concept

Long conversation about the future direction after i.ar alpha completion.
Key decisions:

1. **i.ar is done as an exploratory project.** Alpha complete, all 19 steps done.
   The framework proved its concepts (self-modification, three-axis assembly,
   security model, knowledge bases, autonomous agents). No more framework work.

2. **SecPlatform is a friend's project, not Nacho's passion.** It runs, it works,
   but it's not the interesting path. Don't force productivity over curiosity.

3. **New project: AI Research Laboratory.** A "digital research institution" where
   agents with distinct personalities work alongside the human on open-ended
   research problems. Foundation: co-simulation knowledge base (concept library
   with multi-language implementations). North star: each session, human proposes
   a research problem, agents work on it overnight (or interactively), human
   reviews results.

4. **Framework: LangGraph (Python) as agent runtime.** Not Deep Agents (too
   opinionated about tree delegation), not Mastra (JS/TS, workflow too rigid),
   not Google ADK (too Gemini-optimized). LangGraph gives primitives without
   opinions. Multi-agent layer (message bus, engagement model, threading) is
   custom-built on top.

5. **Chat platform: Zulip.** Self-hosted at agora.randazzo.ar. Stream/topic model
   maps exactly to channel/sub-thread structure. Python bot API (same language
   as LangGraph agents). The chat platform IS the message bus -- no Redis, no
   MQTT. Agents are Zulip bots that subscribe to streams.

6. **No hardcoded workflows.** Agents self-organize through conversation. No
   dependency graphs, no engagement rules, no routing logic. Personalities are
   in prompts, not code. The human wants to be surprised by emergent behavior.

7. **Chat is first-class citizen.** Human interjects in real time, doesn't just
   wake up to results. More hands-on than the "go to sleep" model.

